{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2651 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "3c51aa8c1101971bd2e823bb543dc6feeef4d0786c953af659cead975864f12b" :edb/db-before-t 2650 :edb/db-after-t 2651 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535963 50 #inst "2026-10-09T11:47:19.681Z" 13194139535963 true] [17592186228013 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376944 13194139535963 true] [17592186229136 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376970 13194139535963 true] [17592186229136 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376977 13194139535963 true] [17592186229136 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376984 13194139535963 true] [17592186229136 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376991 13194139535963 true] [17592186229136 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376998 13194139535963 true] [17592186229137 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376945 13194139535963 true] [17592186229137 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376949 13194139535963 true] [17592186229137 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376956 13194139535963 true] [17592186229138 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376963 13194139535963 true] [17592186229139 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376967 13194139535963 true] [17592186229139 1020 #edb/ref 17592186377005 13194139535963 true] [17592186229580 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=8x-4,\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=8x-4\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535963 true] [17592186229580 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{\\prime } - 4y = 8x - 4, y(0) = \\frac{3}{2}$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n$y^{\\prime } - 4y = 8x - 4$\nhas the complementary solution $y_{c} = Ae^{4x}$, solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535963 false] [17592186229580 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -4(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x-4\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha x+(\\alpha -4\\beta )=8x-4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -4\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4(0)}-2(0)+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535963 true] [17592186229580 1145 "Try $y_p=ax+b$. Substitution gives $-4ax+(a-4b)=8x-4$, so $a=-2$ and $b=1/2$. The general solution is $y=Ae^{4x}-2x+1/2$. Applying $y(0)=3/2$ gives $A+1/2=3/2$, hence $A=1$. Therefore $y(x)=e^{4x}-2x+\\frac12$." 13194139535963 false] [17592186376944 1034 "e^{4x}-2x" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376944 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376944 1111 #uuid "e51308c7-7de6-5d87-917d-110f1f0ce9ac" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376945 1017 #edb/ref 17592186376946 13194139535963 true] [17592186376945 1089 "q-332305" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376945 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535963 true] [17592186376945 1100 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=-4x-7.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376945 1113 #uuid "c13ccfab-5d20-52e4-a53a-32d4c8c68460" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376945 1145 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -4(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-4x-7\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha x+(\\alpha -4\\beta )=-4x-7.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -4\\beta =-7\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x+2.\n$$" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376946 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376947 13194139535963 true] [17592186376946 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376948 13194139535963 true] [17592186376946 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535963 true] [17592186376946 1041 "field-1" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376946 1104 #edb/ref 17592186376948 13194139535963 true] [17592186376946 1110 #uuid "1ec864c8-9080-546f-9ff9-8532c22a57a5" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376947 1034 "x+1" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376947 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376947 1111 #uuid "00e7cf25-dbbe-5487-a493-9e7445060fbd" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376948 1034 "x+2" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376948 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376948 1111 #uuid "e3b96e4e-7fa1-5f99-807a-a658874ee80f" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376949 1017 #edb/ref 17592186376950 13194139535963 true] [17592186376949 1089 "q-332323" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376949 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535963 true] [17592186376949 1100 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}-2x-2.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139535963 true] [17592186376949 1113 #uuid "776d5ca9-c850-5a1e-a975-705e11432c48" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376949 1145 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x-2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =0,\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}-2.\n$$" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376951 13194139535963 true] [17592186376950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376952 13194139535963 true] [17592186376950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376953 13194139535963 true] [17592186376950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376954 13194139535963 true] [17592186376950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376955 13194139535963 true] [17592186376950 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535963 true] [17592186376950 1041 "selection" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376950 1104 #edb/ref 17592186376954 13194139535963 true] [17592186376950 1110 #uuid "1532b375-6425-5c93-b0e3-ee7b369f92a0" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376951 1034 "-{x}^{2}-2x-2" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376951 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376951 1111 #uuid "e1a571e7-3b74-5ab5-9e2c-0883e761ede7" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376952 1034 "{x}^{2}-2" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376952 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376952 1111 #uuid "799dfbc0-6a59-5101-b879-6c04e18769f6" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376953 1034 "-{x}^{2}+2" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376953 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376953 1111 #uuid "257b8da1-ffdf-555e-b5a2-1057d77055ee" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376954 1034 "-{x}^{2}-2" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376954 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376954 1111 #uuid "2104400c-db5a-5566-bb1c-c92b5fcd4b60" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376955 1034 "-5{x}^{2}-2" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376955 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376955 1111 #uuid "b69b0cab-6362-5ce6-b6f7-31feaafd6c03" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376956 1017 #edb/ref 17592186376957 13194139535963 true] [17592186376956 1089 "q-332325" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376956 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535963 true] [17592186376956 1100 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y={x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139535963 true] [17592186376956 1113 #uuid "2af28a4b-d570-53bf-8474-03cfab3d34eb" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376956 1145 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )={x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha +2\\beta )x+(2\\beta +2\\gamma )={x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta +2\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =-1,\\gamma =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x.\n$$" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376957 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376958 13194139535963 true] [17592186376957 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376959 13194139535963 true] [17592186376957 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376960 13194139535963 true] [17592186376957 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376961 13194139535963 true] [17592186376957 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376962 13194139535963 true] [17592186376957 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535963 true] [17592186376957 1041 "selection" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376957 1104 #edb/ref 17592186376958 13194139535963 true] [17592186376957 1110 #uuid "c82706d3-e7dd-5e98-b527-d5533e439c95" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376958 1034 "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376958 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376958 1111 #uuid "ea06e80a-8d93-58cc-ba4f-64e85b08492d" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376959 1034 "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376959 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376959 1111 #uuid "85db2297-1a5f-5d99-9781-6143224694a3" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376960 1034 "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x-2" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376960 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376960 1111 #uuid "081d6a19-0fb7-5c09-9804-eceae088cdf1" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376961 1034 "{x}^{2}-2" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376961 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376961 1111 #uuid "4fdd41e7-1716-5080-880c-8d77427fb67b" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376962 1034 "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376962 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376962 1111 #uuid "10a365a2-a606-5373-89a8-8d9d4b2c0b4b" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376963 1017 #edb/ref 17592186376964 13194139535963 true] [17592186376963 1089 "q-332546" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376963 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535963 true] [17592186376963 1100 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=-9.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376963 1113 #uuid "98b14171-a190-5722-ab9c-1b6df5d59394" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376963 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+3\\beta =-9\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =-3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}-3.\n$$" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376964 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376965 13194139535963 true] [17592186376964 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376966 13194139535963 true] [17592186376964 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535963 true] [17592186376964 1041 "field-1" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376964 1104 #edb/ref 17592186376965 13194139535963 true] [17592186376964 1110 #uuid "990c8afb-7e39-535b-a607-326439100b8e" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376965 1034 "Ae^{-3x}-3" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376965 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376965 1111 #uuid "e0db1856-65f8-5ee2-a470-df2611fe28cf" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376966 1034 "Ae^{-3x}+3" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376966 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376966 1111 #uuid "54d4340c-4a94-5c86-9107-747b9d65dfca" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376967 1017 #edb/ref 17592186376968 13194139535963 true] [17592186376967 1089 "q-332558" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376967 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535963 true] [17592186376967 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=4x+6,\\,y(0)=3.\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=4x+6\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376967 1113 #uuid "e09ee532-dc3c-5770-ae00-babef0c316b9" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376967 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x+\\beta )=4x+6\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x+(\\alpha +2\\beta )=4x+6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +2\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x+2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+2x+2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-2(0)}+2(0)+2 & =3 \\\\ A+2 & =3 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}+2x+2.\n$$" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376969 13194139535963 true] [17592186376968 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535963 true] [17592186376968 1041 "field-1" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376968 1104 #edb/ref 17592186376969 13194139535963 true] [17592186376968 1110 #uuid "2f6274f7-31a7-531a-821e-f3a1796a96d7" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376969 1034 "e^{-2x}+2x+2" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376969 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535963 true] [17592186376969 1111 #uuid "2af80dc5-e39b-5b4e-b9ac-64f543786310" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376970 1017 #edb/ref 17592186376971 13194139535963 true] [17592186376970 1089 "q-332642" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376970 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535963 true] [17592186376970 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+6y=-5{x}^{2}+4x-7.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139535963 true] [17592186376970 1113 #uuid "28806fbf-9dcd-5e9c-ad17-59ce05f99707" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376970 1145 "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-6{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-6\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-6x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-6x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-6x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139535963 true] [17592186376971 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376972 13194139535963 true] [17592186376971 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376973 13194139535963 true] [17592186376971 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376974 13194139535963 true] [17592186376971 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376975 13194139535963 true] [17592186376971 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376976 13194139535963 true] [17592186376971 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535963 true] [17592186376971 1041 "selection" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376971 1104 #edb/ref 17592186376972 13194139535963 true] [17592186376971 1110 #uuid "e79819bf-0bc6-5d97-8d52-938c841cfb1e" 13194139535963 true] [17592186376972 1034 "A{e}^{-6x}" 131