y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}+4x-8\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(\\beta -2\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}+4x-8\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =5\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta =4\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }x) \\\\ \\beta -2\\gamma =-8\\,(\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{5}{2},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{9}{2},$ and $\\gamma =\\frac{7}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial condition.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{2(0)}-\\frac{5}{2}(0)^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}(0)+\\frac{7}{4} & =2 \\\\ A+\\frac{7}{4} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535963 true] [17592186377006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377007 13194139535963 true] [17592186377006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377008 13194139535963 true] [17592186377006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377009 13194139535963 true] [17592186377006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377010 13194139535963 true] [17592186377006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377011 13194139535963 true] [17592186377006 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535963 true] [17592186377006 1041 "selection" 13194139535963 true] [17592186377006 1104 #edb/ref 17592186377008 13194139535963 true] [17592186377006 1110 #uuid "